/-!
# ordᵥ(df) = ordᵥ(f) - 1 at zeros and poles

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.Place.ordDiff_D_eq_ord_sub_one`
(`e47cd320-aa83-5adf-89df-8b840fc002f1`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `7191dcfb-0764-5a3c-a366-0fb6b581d97b`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ be a field of characteristic zero, $F$ a field equipped with a $K$-algebra structure, and
suppose some $x \in F$ is such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$
generated by $x$. Let $v$ be a place of $F$ over $K$ in the sense of the structure `Place`, that is,
a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, distinct from all of $F$, and a principal
ideal ring; write $\operatorname{ord}_v$ for the associated normalised integer valuation, obtained
as minus the logarithm of the $\mathbb{Z}^{m0}$-valued adic valuation of the height-one prime of
$v$. For $\omega \in \Omega[F\,⁄\,K]$ put $\operatorname{ordDiff}_v(\omega) =
\operatorname{ord}_v(g)$, where $t$ is a chosen element with $\operatorname{ord}_v(t) = 1$ and $g$
is a chosen element of $F$ with $\omega = g \cdot D_{K/F}(t)$ (both choices default to $0$ if no
such element exists). Then for every $f \in F$ with $\operatorname{ord}_v(f) \neq 0$ one has
$\operatorname{ordDiff}_v(D_{K/F}(f)) = \operatorname{ord}_v(f) - 1$, a single formula covering both
zeros and poles of $f$.

This is the local computation of the order of a differential at a zero or pole, in the form
$\operatorname{ord}_v(df) = \operatorname{ord}_v(f) - 1$ for $\operatorname{ord}_v(f) \neq 0$, valid
in characteristic zero where no $p$-th power phenomenon can occur. It feeds the computation of
canonical divisors and hence the genus of modular curves, being used in the identification of the
genus of the modular function field with the genus formula and in the study of regular differentials
there.

